@meta
id: 01-02
title: Insert before, append after
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: insert-i, insert-a, survival-motion, esc
par: 14
minutes: 4
vscode: clicking to put the caret exactly where you want it
open: standup.md

@brief
`i` puts you in insert mode to the *left* of the cursor. Sooner or later
you need the other side, and the last character of a line has no right
hand side to click on. That is what `a` is for: append, to the right.

One character is missing from the end of the Yesterday line. It should read:

    Yesterday: shipped the checkout fix

Move down to that line, get to its end, and add the missing letter.

Move with `j` for now. `h j k l` are left, down, up, right — chapter 4
makes you fast with them, today they just have to get you there.

@keys
| `j` `k` | down a line, up a line |
| `h` `l` | left a character, right a character |
| `$` | jump to the last character of the line |
| `0` | jump to the first column |
| `a` | append — insert mode, to the right of the cursor |

@hints
Two lines down from the title is a blank line; the Yesterday line is
below that. `j` moves down one line at a time.
--
`$` takes you straight to the last character on the line, no matter how
long it is. You land *on* the final character, not past it.
--
Sitting on the last character, `i` would type before it. You want `a`.
Then the letter, then `Esc`, then `:wq`.

@solution
    jj $ a x <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`$` is doing the real work. The instinct from VS Code is to aim the mouse
at the end of the line, or hold the right arrow key. `$` is one key and it
is exact, whether the line is nine characters or ninety.

`i` and `a` are the first pair of many in vim where a lower-case key and
its variant sit on either side of the cursor. Once you notice the pattern
you stop memorising keys one at a time and start guessing correctly.

